{"id":"crash","title":"Crashing the glass","lead":"How many attackers crash after a shot, what it buys in offensive rebounds, and what it costs in the opponent's next chance.","built":"2026-11-09 12:57","sections":[{"id":"cr-question","title":"The question and the data","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"After a shot goes up, each attacker chooses to go to the glass, hold his spot or get back."},{"type":"list","items":["Crashing wins offensive rebounds. Getting back stops transition","This tab counts those choices on every shot in the ten games, using the site's rebounding roles","Roles are judged during the shot's flight, before the ball comes off the rim","Each live miss is followed to its rebound and to the next chance"]},{"type":"kpis","items":[{"label":"Live misses","value":"741","sub":"of 1,457 shots"},{"label":"Crashers per miss","value":"1.67","sub":"0.87 retreat or safety"},{"label":"Offense keeps the ball","value":"31%","sub":"offensive or team rebound"},{"label":"Opponent after a defensive rebound","value":"0.78 pts","sub":"transition 41%"}]},{"type":"caveat","items":["Roles come from simple distance rules on the tracking (web/scripts/glass.R)","Players getting back are often off camera (81% of their frames detected), so their positions are partly extrapolated"]}]},{"id":"cr-rate","title":"Crash rate and offensive rebounds","blocks":[{"type":"grid","cols":2,"blocks":[{"type":"rates","title":"Offense keeps the ball, by number of crashers","rows":[{"label":"0","n":88,"rate":0.1932,"lo":0.1335,"hi":0.2711},{"label":"1","n":275,"rate":0.3236,"lo":0.2791,"hi":0.3716},{"label":"2","n":205,"rate":0.3122,"lo":0.2617,"hi":0.3676},{"label":"3+","n":173,"rate":0.3526,"lo":0.2955,"hi":0.4142}],"ref":0.3117,"fmt":"pct","unit":"of live misses kept","refLabel":"all","max":null,"min":null,"labelWidth":170,"note":null},{"type":"rates","title":"…by attackers getting back (retreat or safety)","rows":[{"label":"0","n":276,"rate":0.3551,"lo":0.3093,"hi":0.4037},{"label":"1","n":32